Managed by Ansible (teamcraft-deploy, role proxy_snippets).

TeamCraft runs here without its own reverse proxy (caddy_enabled: false). These are configurations for
the proxy you run, filled in with this host's names and ports. Pick the one for your proxy, copy it to
where your proxy reads its configuration, check and reload. The guide: docs/reverse-proxy.md in
teamcraft-deploy.

  nginx/teamcraft.conf     nginx (also behind other panels that let you add server blocks)
  plesk/<host>.conf        Plesk: "Additional nginx directives" of each domain
  apache/teamcraft.conf    Apache 2.4.47+
  haproxy/teamcraft.cfg    HAProxy 2.6+
  traefik/teamcraft.yml    Traefik v3, file provider
  caddy/Caddyfile          another Caddy

Host names and where they go ({{ teamcraft_upstream_host }} is this host's loopback):
{% for site in proxy_snippets_sites %}
{% set kind, host, port = site.split() %}
  https://{{ host }}  ->  http://{{ teamcraft_upstream_host }}:{{ port }}  ({{ kind }})
{% endfor %}
{% if teamcraft_sfu_enabled %}
  {{ teamcraft_sfu_hostname }}  ->  no proxy: the SFU itself on udp/{{ teamcraft_ports.sfu }}, with a copy of this name's
  certificate ({{ teamcraft_cert_dir }}/sfu.crt, from {{ teamcraft_sfu_certificate_nginx_conf or teamcraft_sfu_certificate_path or 'not set' }})
{% endif %}

Every proxy must: pass Host unchanged; set X-Forwarded-For (the client's address), -Proto and -Host;
upgrade WebSockets on /api/v1/gateway and /api/v1/rendezvous with a read timeout over 30 s; refuse
/api/internal/; accept request bodies up to 4 MiB on the server's names and any size on S3's.
